2|0@short^1439996657#cu8axth:bvcxy$:No one "avoids" heightism. There's this preconception here that heightism only affects short guys. It does not. It affects tall guys (positively), tall women (positively or negatively depending on their body type), short women (positively or negatively) and short guys (mostly negatively) as well. Heightism can affect average people who're expected to grow tall (when they're kids) or when they try out in sports in which a much taller height is the norm (so they get the same treatment as short guys in the real world despite being average). Heightism, much like racism affects everyone, but it does not affect everyone equally. 
1|0@short^1440010646#cu8kpj0:TahVeryMuch$:Obviously it will vary by country, but my personal and evidence-lacking opinion is that anything greater than 1 inch below the norm is where heightism can start to occur. Having said that, I'm 5'6.5" (UK) and I can't remember ever being openly discriminated against because of my height, although I often feel like I'm pressured into being the one who has to move out of the way of people in crowds.
1|0@short^1439995202#cu8a1gg:DevilishRogue$:It's obviously relative depending on context. There is no black and white cut off and even tall people can experience heightism in certain situations (and in some ways it's good that they do because it makes them more understanding and appreciative of the realities faced by their fellows). 
0|0@short^1439956018#cu7vzxt:mnt68$:A guy can't really avoid heightism. You can be taller than the majority of other guys in your community and only date women who are taller than average, and you can still have offspring which are shorter than average. Those offspring will face heightism, so the father faces having to deal with heightism.
